# WimForge -- line endings.
#
# This matters for one file type in particular. The two Explorer launchers are
# batch, and batch is parsed line by line by cmd.exe: a .cmd checked out with
# bare LF endings can mis-parse a label or a parenthesised block, which shows up
# as a launcher that does nothing rather than as an error. Anyone cloning this on
# a machine with core.autocrlf=input would get exactly that, so the endings are
# pinned here instead of trusted to whoever clones it.
#
# Setting this BEFORE the first commit costs nothing. Adding it to a repository
# with history renormalises every file and shows up as one enormous diff, so if
# this ever needs changing, change it in its own commit.

# Let git decide for anything not named below, storing LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# Checked out with CRLF: everything Windows runs directly.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.xml  text eol=crlf
*.reg  text eol=crlf

# Markdown, JSON and the licence are read by tools that do not care, so they
# follow the platform.
*.md   text

# YAML is whitespace-significant and GitHub Actions reads it on Linux runners,
# so it is pinned to LF regardless of what the working copy is checked out with.
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text

# Never touched, never diffed. Most of these are ignored anyway; named here so
# that one forced in with -f cannot be mangled on the way through.
*.wim  binary
*.esd  binary
*.swm  binary
*.iso  binary
*.msu  binary
*.cab  binary
*.ppkg binary
*.sys  binary
*.cat  binary
*.dll  binary
*.pfx  binary
*.sdi  binary
Registry.pol binary
